        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Where are Landice treadmills made?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Landice is based in Randolph, New Jersey, and says it makes and assembles its treadmills in the USA, building each to order. It is a family-controlled private company founded in 1967. Landice does not publish where every component comes from, so if domestic sourcing matters to you, ask a dealer which parts are made where.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does a Landice treadmill cost?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On 14 October 2026 Landice listed the folding M2 Genesis at $3,999, the L7 from $5,429 and the L8 from $6,329. Those are the lowest configurations. With the 15.6-inch touchscreen console the L7 was $7,229 and the L8 $8,129. Delivery and installation terms vary with promotions, so confirm the delivered price before ordering.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between the Landice L7 and L8?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The L8 has a larger running area, 22 by 63 inches against 20 by 58, and a 500 lb user rating against 400 lb. Motor rating, speed, incline, console choices and residential warranty are the same. The L8 is seven inches longer overall and costs about $900 more at the lowest configurations we saw.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does the Landice warranty really cover parts for life?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For the residential L7 and L8, Landice’s warranty page gives lifetime parts, five years on console parts and one year of labour. Labour applies only when a dealer installs the machine within 60 driving miles of its nearest store. You must register within 30 days, and cover belongs to the original owner. The M2 and club models carry different terms.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does Landice make a folding treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, one: the M2 Genesis. Landice lists a 21 by 62-inch running area, a 300 lb user rating, 15% incline and a 15.6-inch touchscreen. It folds to 55 inches long but stays 66 inches tall and weighs about 290 lb. It was listed at $3,999 on 14 October 2026. The L-Series models do not fold.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I see a Landice treadmill before buying?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, at a Landice dealer or some Scheels stores. When we checked, scheels.com listed the L7 as available in store only, so a Scheels visit is one of the easier ways to walk on one. Landice’s dealer locator lists specialist stores, and buying locally matters because residential labour cover depends on dealer installation.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
